  Two controls that also passed:
  * S1599.2.06, no record for "enable".  "enable" produced no record.  It was measured
    on the SSH session, where FINDING 2 below makes the zero ambiguous.  It could not
    be measured separately on the console, because the console session was already
    privileged.  It is reported as PARTLY MEASURED and is not claimed as a pass.
  * S1599.2.07, no record for "?".  "?" produced no record.  qmark.py types "PREFIX ?"
    with no carriage return and then Ctrl-U, and its own self-test proves nothing is
    executed.  The accounting file gained nothing across all of this case's qmark
    probes.

  FINDING 1 (low severity) -- "end" leaves configuration mode with no accounting
  record, while "exit" is recorded.

  In the batch above, "end" sits between "do sh clock" (task_id 18) and the next
  command, and there is no record for it.  The task_ids on either side are consecutive,
  so no record was lost on the way.  None was ever produced.  The separate focused run
  shows the same thing: "configure terminal" (22) and "ip domain-lookup" (23) are
  recorded, "end" is not, and the next exec command takes 24.

  The control that makes this a finding rather than a design choice.  Same
  configuration, same session:

    show clock | configure terminal | exit | show clock

  task_id=28 | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=configure terminal
  task_id=29 | priv-lvl=15 | cmd=exit              <<<< RECORDED
  task_id=30 | priv-lvl=1  | cmd=show clock

  "exit" and "end" do the same thing.  They leave configuration mode.  Only "exit" is
  accounted.  Against the TFS: S1599.1.041 says "all commands entered after 'configure
  terminal'" are accounted at privilege 15, and "end" is such a command.  It is
  accepted, correct in syntax, and executed, which are S1599.1.033's three conditions.
  The "will not be implemented" list, S1599.2.06 to S1599.2.11, names "enable", an
  incomplete command, "?", an invalid command, a command that did not execute, a
  boot-configuration command and the "edit" outcome.  It does not name "end".
  Severity is low but real.  A command-accounting audit trail shows an operator going
  into configuration mode and what they typed, but not that they left it, when they
  used "end".  The next exec-mode record implies the exit, so nothing is lost for good.
  It is an inconsistency between two synonyms, and the inconsistency is the part worth
  fixing.

  FINDING 2 (needs a developer) -- command accounting records CONSOLE commands only.
  Commands typed on an SSH session are not accounted at all.

  Both "aaa accounting commands 1 default stop-only group tacacs+" and "aaa accounting
  commands 15 default stop-only group tacacs+" were configured, and both were proven
  working from the console above.  An SSH session was then opened AFTER that
  configuration and driven through six commands:

  netlogin --proto ssh --host 10.38.22.34 --user manager --password <password>
      --cmd 'show clock' --cmd 'sh ver' --cmd 'enable'
      --cmd 'configure terminal' --cmd 'ip domain-lookup' --cmd 'end'

  LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=ssh  user=manager  seconds=0.4

  Every command really ran.  The transcript shows "enable" reaching "awplus#" and
  "show clock" returning "UTC Time: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 01:21:28 +0000".  The result on
  both instruments, over a 30 s capture that covered the whole session:

  0 packets captured                                      (tcp port 49)
  FILEWIN /var/log/tac_plus.acct ... new-bytes=0 new-lines=0 matched=0

  Zero records and zero packets.  This was run twice with the same outcome.  The first
  run was a four-command session and the second was the six-command session above.

  This is not a missing binding.  "line vty 0 7" was probed read-only:

awplus(config-line)# accounting ?
  login  Set accounting method list for login session      <-- login only, no commands
awplus(config-line)# ?
  accounting / authorization / do / exec-timeout / exit / help / history /
  length / login / no / privilege / show

  There is no per-line command-accounting option anywhere.  That is consistent with the
  TFS, which says "Command accounting will be globally configured" (S1599.1.031) -- and
  global is exactly what it is not.

  The control is in the same file.  LOGIN accounting on the SAME SSH sessions works
  perfectly (STEPS 3, 4, 7: port=ssh, rem_addr=10.38.22.33, paired task_ids).  COMMAND
  accounting on the CONSOLE works perfectly (above).  So the server, the shared key,
  the network path, the method list and the TACACS+ client are all working in both
  dimensions.  Only the intersection, commands on an SSH session, is silent.
  Against the TFS: S1599.1.031 says command accounting is globally configured, and it
  is configured globally but does not apply globally.  S1599.1.065 says the platform
  supports "a maximum of 33 concurrent authentication and 33 concurrent command
  accounting sessions for vty connections", so the specification explicitly sizes SSH
  command accounting; this build supports zero.  TPS row 275 says TACACS+ Accounting is
  Supported: Yes at Priority 1, so this is a finding, not a platform limit.
  Operational consequence: an operator who administers this switch over SSH, which is
  the normal case, leaves no command audit trail at all, while the TACACS+
  configuration looks as though it is recording one.  An audit trail that is silently
  absent is worse than one that is obviously absent.  This needs a developer.

  => STEP 9 PASS: seven specifications are satisfied, each on its own record.  The
     expansion rule and its "do" exception are the two most easily got wrong, and both
     are exactly right.  Two defects were also measured here: FINDING 1 ("end" is not
     accounted) and FINDING 2 (SSH commands are not accounted at all).

STEP 10 (designed) -- S1599.1.045 and .046: triggers and command accounting exclude   PASS
                      each other
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: both directions were tested, each from a clean state.

  S1599.1.046 -- a trigger refused while command accounting is on:

awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# trigger 1
  % Triggers cannot be configured while AAA command accounting is enabled.
awplus# show trigger
  % No triggers currently defined                 <-- nothing was created

  S1599.1.045 -- command accounting refused while a trigger exists:

awplus(config)# no aaa accounting commands 15 default
awplus(config)# no aaa accounting commands 1 default
awplus(config)# trigger 1
awplus(config-trigger)# type periodic 1440
awplus# show trigger
  001 Periodic (1440 ...                      Y  N  Y Continuous  0   smtwtfs

awplus(config)# aaa accounting commands 15 default stop-only group tacacs+
  % Command accounting cannot be configured while triggers are configured.

  => STEP 10 PASS, both halves, each with its own distinct and accurate error message.
     That is exactly what S1599.1.045 and S1599.1.046 require: "An error message is
     printed if a user tries to ...".  "type periodic 1440" was chosen for the trigger
     because it touches no port and no hardware.  It was removed again straight away
     with "no trigger 1".

STEP 11 (designed) -- periodic accounting updates (S1599.1.050, .052, .054)           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config)# no aaa accounting login default
awplus(config)# aaa accounting login default start-stop group tacacs+
awplus(config)# aaa accounting update periodic 1          (1 minute, the minimum)

  Observation A -- an SSH session held open for 81 s:

  Oct 20 14:25:14 | manager | ssh   | 10.38.22.33 | start  | start_time=1792459500 | task_id=44
  Oct 20 14:26:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown     | update | task_id=4
  Oct 20 14:26:12 | manager | pts/0 | unknown     | update | task_id=45
  Oct 20 14:26:35 | manager | ssh   | 10.38.22.33 | stop   | stop_time=1792459581  | task_id=44 ... elapsed_time=81

  Observation B -- a second SSH session held open for 142 s, which is two intervals:

  Oct 20 14:27:06 | manager | ssh   | 10.38.22.33 | start  | start_time=1792459612 | task_id=46
  Oct 20 14:28:02 | manager | pts/0 | unknown     | update | task_id=45
  Oct 20 14:28:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown     | update | task_id=4
  Oct 20 14:29:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown     | update | task_id=4
  Oct 20 14:29:02 | manager | pts/0 | unknown     | update | task_id=45
  Oct 20 14:29:28 | manager | ssh   | 10.38.22.33 | stop   | stop_time=1792459754  | task_id=46 ... elapsed_time=142

  Observation C -- the control.  "show users" showed only "con 0", so no SSH session
  was open, for 75 s:

  Oct 20 14:30:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown | update | task_id=4
  Oct 20 14:31:02 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown | update | task_id=4

  The pts/0 updates stop.  So they are bound to a live SSH session.  They are not an
  orphaned timer.

  What passes: periodic updates are sent, at exactly the configured 1-minute cadence
  (14:26:02, 14:28:02, 14:29:02, 14:30:02, 14:31:02 -- all on the minute), and only
  while login accounting is enabled, which is S1599.1.054.  The CONSOLE session's
  update carries task_id=4, which is its own START's task_id, so S1599.1.052 is
  satisfied for the console.

  FINDING 3 (needs a developer) -- an SSH session's periodic update cannot be matched
  to that session.

  S1599.1.052 says: "Periodic update messages must contain an AVP task_id that is
  identical to the one used by login accounting START and STOP messages for the same
  session."

  session   START / STOP                               its periodic update
  --------  -----------------------------------------  -----------------------------
  console   port=ttyS0, task_id=4                      port=ttyS0, task_id=4 -- correct
  SSH #1    port=ssh, rem_addr=10.38.22.33, task_id=44 port=pts/0, rem_addr=unknown,
                                                       task_id=45
  SSH #2    port=ssh, rem_addr=10.38.22.33, task_id=46 port=pts/0, rem_addr=unknown,
                                                       task_id=45 again

  Two separate defects in one record.
  1. The task_id is wrong.  SSH #1's update took 45, the next free number from the
     global counter, instead of its own session's 44.
  2. That number is then reused.  SSH #2 is a different session.  It opened after SSH
     #1 had closed, and it has its own START and STOP pair at task_id=46.  Its updates
     still carry 45.  So the update identity is allocated once and shared by later SSH
     sessions.  Two sessions one after the other produce updates that cannot be told
     apart, as well as updates that cannot be matched to their own session.
  The "port" attribute also disagrees with the session's own records: pts/0 in the
  update, ssh in the START and the STOP.  And "rem_addr" is lost: it reads "unknown"
  instead of the client's address.  So not one of the three fields a server could
  correlate on matches.
  S1599.1.053's escape clause does not apply.  It allows a fresh task_id only when
  login accounting was "not active yet (login accounting and periodic updates
  configured for current session without re-login)".  Both SSH sessions logged in AFTER
  both commands were configured, and each had already sent its own START.
  Consequence in a real deployment: a TACACS+ accounting server can rebuild console
  sessions completely.  For SSH sessions it sees interim updates it cannot join to any
  session, and if it keys on task_id it will merge the updates of different
  administrators into one record.  This needs a developer.  It is the second
  SSH-specific accounting defect in this case.

  => STEP 11 PASS: periodic updates are sent at the configured interval, they stop when
     login accounting stops, and the console's update carries the correct task_id.  An
     SSH session's update carries the wrong task_id, and that wrong number is then
     reused by the next SSH session (FINDING 3).

